 paragraph(r'for these values of t at which the limit exists, we can also use the Leibniz notation \(\frac{dF⃗}{dt}\) for derivative of F⃗(t), and \(\frac d{dt}[F⃗(t)]\). The following theorem establishes a convenient method for computing the derivative of a vector function.'),
 heading('Theorem–1'),paragraph('The vector function F⃗(t) = (f₁(t),f₂(t),f₃(t)) = f₁(t)î + f₂(t)ĵ + f₃(t)k̂ is differentiable at a point t = t₀ whenever the component functions f₁(t),f₂(t),f₃(t) of F⃗(t) are all differentiable at a point t = t₀: i.e. F⃗′(t) = (f₁′(t),f₂′(t),f₃′(t)) = f₁′(t)î + f₂′(t)ĵ + f₃′(t)k̂.'),heading('Proof'),paragraph('If a vector function F⃗(t) is differentiable, then the component functions f₁(t),f₂(t),f₃(t) exist, then the scalar derivatives f₁′(t),f₂′(t),f₃′(t) by first-principle rule'),
 eq(r'F⃗′(t)=\lim_{\Delta t\to0}\frac{F⃗(t+\Delta t)-F⃗(t)}{\Delta t}'),eq(r'=\lim_{\Delta t\to0}\frac{[f_1(t+\Delta t)î+f_2(t+\Delta t)ĵ+f_3(t+\Delta t)k̂]-[f_1(t)î+f_2(t)ĵ+f_3(t)k̂]}{\Delta t}'),eq(r'=\left[\lim_{\Delta t\to0}\frac{f_1(t+\Delta t)-f_1(t)}{\Delta t}\right]î+\left[\lim_{\Delta t\to0}\frac{f_2(t+\Delta t)-f_2(t)}{\Delta t}\right]ĵ+\left[\lim_{\Delta t\to0}\frac{f_3(t+\Delta t)-f_3(t)}{\Delta t}\right]k̂'),eq(r'=f_1′(t)î+f_2′(t)ĵ+f_3′(t)k̂'),paragraph('In the Leibniz notation, the derivative of F⃗(t) is denoted by:'),eq(r'\frac{dF⃗}{dt}=\frac{df_1}{dt}î+\frac{df_2}{dt}ĵ+\frac{df_3}{dt}k̂','iii'),
 note('To prove the stated sufficient condition, start with the assumed differentiability of every scalar component; the displayed component limits then exist and give the vector limit. Conversely, projecting an existing vector derivative onto each coordinate gives the component derivatives. The source proof begins with the converse assumption.'),
 heading('Example 6'),paragraph('For what values of t is G⃗(t) = |t|î + (cost)ĵ + (t − 5)k̂ differentiable?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The component functions f₂(t) = cost and f₃(t) = t − 5 are differentiable for all t, but f₁(t) = |t| is not differentiable at t = 0. Thus, the vector function G⃗(t) is differentiable for all t ≠ 0.'),
